provider is unpublished. Unset `TF_CLI_CONFIG_FILE` when finished using the override.

## Authentication and Configuration

Credentials are required when a resource or data source makes an API request.
Provider configuration contacts Catalyst only when `validation_project_id` is set.
The provider does not
read the Catalyst CLI login, automatically load `.env` files, register an OAuth
client or run interactive browser consent.

Explicit provider arguments take precedence over environment variables. A null
or omitted argument falls back to its environment variable; an explicit empty
string suppresses the fallback. Configuration values must be known before apply.

Managed resources require `organization_id` or `CATALYST_ORGANIZATION_ID`.
Environment-scoped resources save their region, environment and organization in
`routing_scope`. Changing provider routing does not move resources or promote them
to Production. Read the [environment isolation and migration guide](guides/environments.md)
before refreshing legacy state or configuring a second environment.

### OAuth Refresh Credentials

Register an OAuth client for your account's data center, obtain a grant with the
required service scopes, then exchange it for refresh credentials. Follow the
official [client registration](https://docs.catalyst.zoho.com/en/api/oauth2/register-new-client/),
[grant token](https://docs.catalyst.zoho.com/en/api/oauth2/generate-grant-token/) and
[token exchange](https://docs.catalyst.zoho.com/en/api/oauth2/generate-access-and-refresh-token/)
instructions. The provider consumes existing credentials.

```shell
export CATALYST_DATA_CENTER="in"
export CATALYST_ENVIRONMENT="Development"
export CATALYST_ORGANIZATION_ID="your-organization-id"
export CATALYST_CLIENT_ID="your-client-id"
export CATALYST_CLIENT_SECRET="your-client-secret"
export CATALYST_REFRESH_TOKEN="your-refresh-token"
unset CATALYST_ACCESS_TOKEN
```

Set `CATALYST_REDIRECT_URI` when your OAuth client requires its registered redirect
URI; omit it for a Self Client. Access tokens are refreshed automatically and
cached in memory for each provider instance. Independent Terraform processes have
separate caches. Service-specific scopes are listed on each resource/data source
page; project-read permission alone does not authorize deployments.

Refresh requests retry explicit HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503 and 504 responses up to
three times using exponential backoff with jitter. `Retry-After` is respected; a
requested delay above 30 seconds is returned as an error rather than retried early.
The complete refresh sequence has a 30-second deadline and is shared by concurrent
callers. Credential rejection, malformed responses and ambiguous transport failures
are not automatically replayed. Resource writes retain their separate retry policy.

Transient OAuth failures retain a retry deadline for that provider instance,
including server delays longer than the refresh deadline. New callers fail fast
during cooldown without contacting the token endpoint; callers already sharing a
refresh remain attached to its bounded retry sequence. Cancelling all callers or
reaching the refresh deadline does not discard an observed cooldown. After retry
exhaustion without a usable `Retry-After`, the last jittered backoff becomes the
cooldown. Oversized numeric delays fail closed instead of overflowing. A successful
refresh clears the cooldown. Valid cached tokens remain usable, and other aliases
have independent cooldowns. Deadlines are held in memory, not persisted across
Terraform processes.

### Access Token

For a short-lived session, use an access token instead of refresh credentials:

```shell
export CATALYST_DATA_CENTER="in"
export CATALYST_ACCESS_TOKEN="your-access-token"
unset CATALYST_CLIENT_ID CATALYST_CLIENT_SECRET CATALYST_REFRESH_TOKEN CATALYST_REDIRECT_URI
```

Access-token mode does not refresh automatically. Mixed authentication modes and
incomplete refresh credentials are rejected. Keep credentials outside committed
Terraform configuration and application archives.

### Environment Variables

| Provider argument | Environment variable | Behavior when omitted |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `data_center` | `CATALYST_DATA_CENTER` | `us` in Development; explicit value required in Production |
| `environment` | `CATALYST_ENVIRONMENT` | `Development` |
| `organization_id` | `CATALYST_ORGANIZATION_ID` | Required for managed resources; optional for data sources |
| `access_token` | `CATALYST_ACCESS_TOKEN` | Use complete refresh credentials, if supplied |
| `client_id` | `CATALYST_CLIENT_ID` | Required with refresh authentication |
| `client_secret` | `CATALYST_CLIENT_SECRET` | Required with refresh authentication |
| `refresh_token` | `CATALYST_REFRESH_TOKEN` | Required with refresh authentication |
| `redirect_uri` | `CATALYST_REDIRECT_URI` | Not sent |
| `allowed_organization_ids` | `CATALYST_ALLOWED_ORGANIZATION_IDS` | No allowlist; environment accepts comma-separated IDs |
| `forbidden_organization_ids` | `CATALYST_FORBIDDEN_ORGANIZATION_IDS` | No denylist; environment accepts comma-separated IDs |
| `validation_project_id` | `CATALYST_VALIDATION_PROJECT_ID` | No API preflight |
| `allowed_data_centers` | `CATALYST_ALLOWED_DATA_CENTERS` | No regional allowlist; environment accepts comma-separated codes |
| `allowed_environments` | `CATALYST_ALLOWED_ENVIRONMENTS` | No environment allowlist; environment accepts comma-separated names |

### Data Centers, Environments and Aliases

`data_center` accepts `us`, `eu`, `in`, `au`, `ca`, `jp` and `sa`. Select the data
center hosting your account. The provider uses regional API and OAuth endpoints;
an account's data center is independent of your physical location. Regional
service availability and live verification are separate from endpoint support.

`environment` selects `Development` or `Production`. Production requires
`data_center` in provider configuration or `CATALYST_DATA_CENTER`; it cannot use
the implicit US default. An explicit empty region suppresses the environment
fallback and therefore fails in Production. This is a deliberate compatibility
change: existing Production configurations relying on the default must set `us`
explicitly. Selecting Production is not
a deployment promotion or project migration. `organization_id` sets the
`CATALYST-ORG` header and is required for managed resources; it remains optional
for data sources.

```terraform
provider "catalyst" {
  alias       = "development"
  data_center = "in"
  environment = "Development"
}

data "catalyst_project" "example" {
  provider = catalyst.development
  id       = "12345678901234567"
}
```

Aliases inherit environment credentials unless explicitly configured otherwise.
Use sensitive input variables for aliases needing different credentials; the
process environment is shared.

### Regional Security Policies

Use `allowed_data_centers` to independently restrict the destination selected by
`data_center`, and `allowed_environments` to restrict `environment`. Nonempty
policies require explicit selected values, supplied through provider arguments or
their environment variables; implicit US/Development defaults are not accepted.
Invalid, null or unknown policy entries fail closed. A rejected policy fails
before OAuth refresh, access preflight or resource requests.

```terraform
provider "catalyst" {
  data_center          = var.data_center
  environment          = "Production"
  allowed_data_centers = ["in"]
  allowed_environments = ["Production"]
}
```

Maintain approved destinations independently from the selected destination in
reviewed configuration or CI inputs. As with organization guardrails, these are
local deployment safety checks, not server-side authorization or proof of data
residency. Explicit sets override environment policy values, and explicit empty
sets suppress those fallbacks. Use nonempty input validation when policies must
always be enabled; see `examples/provider_guardrails`.

The provider uses fixed regional HTTPS hosts and never automatically fails over
to another data center. Standard HTTP transports enforce certificate/hostname
verification and TLS 1.2 or newer; TLS verification bypasses and incompatible
protocol limits are rejected. A stronger supplied TLS minimum is preserved.
Shared transport configurations are cloned before security settings are applied.
There is no Terraform argument to disable verification or set custom API hosts.

OAuth/API redirects and signed-transfer redirects are not followed. Stratus
bucket origins must exactly match the selected region and environment; arbitrary
response URLs are not used as transfer destinations. OAuth and organization
headers are never sent to signed Stratus data hosts. These boundaries have
adversarial offline coverage for every supported region and both environments;
live verification is tracked separately in `API_COVERAGE.md`. Contributors can
run `make testacc-regional-tls` to verify public API/OAuth certificate chains,
hostnames and TLS versions without credentials. This transport probe does not
verify authenticated regional API access.

### Organization Guardrails and Access Preflight

Use a nonempty `allowed_organization_ids` or `forbidden_organization_ids` set to
reject an unintended configured organization before requesting any token or
calling an API. Both nonempty sets cannot be used together. Guardrails require
an explicit `organization_id`, since the token owner's default organization is
not known locally. IDs must be positive integers expressed as strings. Explicit
empty sets disable their environment fallbacks. Unknown or null elements fail
closed.

These guardrails check the configured `CATALYST-ORG` routing header. They do not
discover or attest the authenticated caller's identity. Catalyst's documented
[multi-organization contract](https://docs.catalyst.zoho.com/en/api/introduction/multi-org-support/)
uses that header to select the organization; it does not provide AWS STS-style
caller identity through this provider.

Set `validation_project_id` to read a known existing project during provider
configuration, before clients are supplied to resources or data sources. The
check uses the selected region, environment, organization and authentication mode.
It fails on denied access, mismatched project IDs or malformed responses. It
requires `ZOHOCATALYST.projects.Read` and introduces a network dependency during
configuration. It does not verify every service scope, ownership, or write access.

```terraform
provider "catalyst" {
  data_center              = "in"
  environment              = "Production"
  organization_id          = var.organization_id
  allowed_organization_ids = ["12345678901234567"] # Your approved organization ID
  validation_project_id    = var.validation_project_id
}
```

Supply OAuth credentials through the documented environment variables or
sensitive input variables. Each alias has its own routing, refresh sequence and
token cache; no credential cache is shared between aliases or Terraform processes.
Offline tests cover actual request routing for all seven regions in both
environments. The opt-in `make testacc-regional-auth` target can verify project
reads with independent credentials per region; see `CONTRIBUTING.md`. Offline
coverage does not establish live service availability in every region.

